  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 250 AM CDT Sat Jul 25 2026\n\nThe upper-level ridge will still be holding over the western CONUS \non Monday per guidance consensus, with the worst of the thermal \nridge for the period shifting over the Middle Mississippi Valley. \nThis will result in 850 mb temperatures peaking around 25 degrees C, \nwhich supports surface temperatures in the upper 90s to around 100 \ndegrees. With very little ensemble spread and a warm start to the \nday, confidence is high in this degree of heat. With dew points \nstill expected to be in the mid to upper 70s, heat index values will \ntop out in the 105-110 range during the afternoon for much of the \narea. \n\nConfidence decreases in forecast specifics starting Tuesday as a \nshortwave moving through the Ohio Valley sends a backdoor cold front \ntoward and/or through the CWA. This will result in the thermal ridge \nbreaking down, but guidance varies in how quickly this happens. A \nmajority of guidance supports a decrease in afternoon temperatures \nTuesday compared to Monday, but the speed of the front will dictate \nhow much of the area experiences relief, with a slower front \nfavoring areas along and south of I-70 seeing another day of 100+ \ndegree heat index values. \n\nFor Wednesday into the weekend, the upper-level ridge will remain \ncentered over the Rockies as northwesterly flow persists over much \nof the Midwest. Within this flow, deterministic guidance depicts \nmultiple shortwaves diving southeastward through the region. These \ndisturbances will keep the thermal ridge focused west of the CWA, \nallowing for temperatures to remain closer to seasonal normals \n(highs around 90 degrees). Each of these disturbances will be \ncapable of producing rainfall; however, there is a lack of consensus \namong guidance on if/when rainfall will occur across the CWA, \nleading to low confidence and a mostly dry forecast for this portion \nof the period.",
